This software implements base station protocol of classic mobile phones. With
radio transmitter and receiver connected to one sound card and a headset
connected to another sound card, it is possible to make and receive calls to
and from mobile phone. With SDR (software defined radio) it can be use without
radio equipment connected to a sound card, and even multiple channels can be
generated simultaniously using SDR. Currently supported networks:

 * A-Netz
 * B-Netz (ATF-1)
 * C-Netz
 * NMT 450 / 900 (Nordic Mobile Telephone)
 * AMPS (Advanced Mobile Phone System)
 * TACS (Total Access Communication System)
 * JTACS (Japanese version of TACS)
 * Radiocom 2000 (French network)
 * IMTS / MTS ((Improved) Mobile Telephone Service)
 * MPT1327 (Trunked Radio) aka known as 'Buendelfunk'
 * Eurosignal (ERuRD paging service)
 * 5-Ton-Folge (ZVEI 5 tone paging code for pager and siren trigger)
 * JollyCom (Unofficial network, invented by the author)
 * C-Netz BSC (Connecting to a C-Netz Base Station)

Additionally the following communication services are implemented:

 * TV Transmitter with test Images
 * Radio transmitter / receiver
 * Analog Modem Emulation 'Datenklo' (AM7911)
 * German classic 'Zeitansage' (talking clock)
 * POCSAG transmitter / receiver
 * Golay/GSC transmitter / receiver
 * DCF77 time signal transmitter and receiver with weather info
   (also WWVB, MSF, JJY40, JJY60)
 * C-Netz SIM emulator
 * C-Netz magnetic card emulator
 * FLEX paging transmitter / receiver
 * ERMES paging transmitter (see docs/ERMES.md)
 * Mobitex transmitter / receiver
 * FM broadcast radio with stereo and RDS (osmoradio, see docs/OSMORADIO.md)
 * SDR clock calibration from GSM FCCH (osmocalibrate)

USE AT YOUR OWN RISK!


This tree
=========

This is a fork of osmocom-analog by Andreas Eversberg
(https://gitea.osmocom.org/cellular-infrastructure/osmocom-analog), kept
merged with upstream. In addition to upstream it has:

 * FLEX, Mobitex, more POCSAG and Golay/GSC, a shared message FIFO for the
   paging transmitters (--fifo / --fifo-file)
 * osmoradio: more RDS (RT+, TMC, paging), XDR-GTK protocol
 * NMT: SMS and DMS work, message waiting indicator, moving calls between
   voice channels (docs/NMT_*.md)
 * AMPS / TACS work (docs/AMPS_IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md)
 * SDR: separate TX and RX devices (--sdr-tx-* / --sdr-rx-*), channelizer,
   polyphase resampler, PPM correction, upconverter, and the Raspberry Pi as
   transmitter through rpitx (docs/RPITX.md)
 * DTMF and flash towards the network: RFC 4733 telephone-event in RTP,
   SIP INFO as fallback
 * echo suppressor, DTMF towards the mobile


Installation
============

For Debian, Ubuntu and Raspberry Pi OS. Everything is typed in a terminal, one
line at a time; a line starting with "sudo" asks for your password. Each step
ends with a check: do not go on until it gives what is shown. The upstream
guide is at http://osmocom-analog.eversberg.eu/docs/install.html

Three programs are built, in this order, because each one needs the one before:

  libosmo-cc         the call control library
  osmocom-analog     the base stations and transmitters (this tree)
  osmo-cc-sip-endpoint   optional: calls to a SIP PBX or provider

Step 1. Install the tools and libraries

     sudo apt update
     sudo apt install build-essential autoconf automake libtool pkg-config git
     sudo apt install libosmocore-dev

   For the hardware you have (configure finds them by itself; skip what you do
   not need):

     sudo apt install libasound2-dev                 # sound card (radio, headset)
     sudo apt install libsoapysdr-dev soapysdr-tools # SoapySDR (LimeSDR, ...)
     sudo apt install libuhd-dev uhd-host            # UHD (Ettus USRP)
     sudo apt install libfuse3-dev                   # 'Datenklo' modem emulation
     sudo apt install libmagickcore-dev              # TV images other than PPM
     sudo apt install libsofia-sip-ua-dev            # only for step 5 (SIP)

   Raspberry Pi as transmitter: librpitx, see docs/RPITX.md.

   Check:

     pkg-config --modversion libosmocore

   prints a version (1.6.0 or newer).

Step 2. Get the sources

   A folder for them, here ~/src:

     mkdir -p ~/src
     cd ~/src
     git clone https://gitea.osmocom.org/cc/libosmo-cc
     git clone https://github.com/VasylSamoilov/osmocom-analog
     git clone https://github.com/VasylSamoilov/osmo-cc-sip-endpoint   # only for SIP

   Check: "ls ~/src" lists the folders.

Step 3. Build and install libosmo-cc (3.2.0 or newer)

     cd ~/src/libosmo-cc
     autoreconf -if
     ./configure
     make
     sudo make install
     sudo ldconfig

   Check:

     pkg-config --modversion libosmocc

   prints 3.2.0 or newer (e.g. 3.2.0.4-531f). An older version (3.0.0) means an
   old libosmo-cc is still installed: run the step again in this folder, and
   check that "sudo make install" ended without an error.

Step 4. Build and install osmocom-analog

     cd ~/src/osmocom-analog
     autoreconf -if
     ./configure
     make
     sudo make install

   configure ends with what it found, for example:

     configure: Compiling with Alsa support
     configure: Compiling with UHD SDR support
     configure: Compiling with SoapySDR support
     configure: Compiling with FUSE

   A line missing means its library of step 1 is not installed: install it and
   run "./configure" and "make" again.

   Check:

     bnetz -h

   prints the options of the B-Netz base station.

   The FLEX pager transmitter is "flexpager" (not "flex": that is the scanner
   generator of the Debian package flex). Its settings file is
   ~/.osmocom/analog/flex.conf.

Step 5. Optional: osmo-cc-sip-endpoint (calls through SIP)

   Connects the base stations to a SIP PBX or provider. Upstream
   (https://gitea.osmocom.org/cc/osmo-cc-sip-endpoint) works; this fork also
   sends and receives hook flash as SIP INFO, for peers without RFC 4733
   telephone-event (with it, flash goes in RTP).

     cd ~/src/osmo-cc-sip-endpoint
     autoreconf -if
     ./configure
     make
     sudo make install

   The build warns about a Sofia-SIP patch; see the warning in src/sip/sip.c.

   Check:

     osmo-cc-sip-endpoint -h

   prints its options.


Updating
========

Update in the same order as the installation: libosmo-cc first, then the
programs that use it. Rebuild a program after its library changed: libosmo-cc
3.2.0 changed its interface, and a program built against an older one does not
link ("undefined reference to osmo_cc_...").

     cd ~/src/libosmo-cc
     git pull
     make clean
     autoreconf -if
     ./configure
     make
     sudo make install
     sudo ldconfig

     cd ~/src/osmocom-analog
     git pull
     make clean
     autoreconf -if
     ./configure
     make
     sudo make install

   and the same for osmo-cc-sip-endpoint. "make clean" matters: without it make
   keeps parts built against the old library.


When something goes wrong
=========================

 * configure: "Package requirements (libosmocc >= 3.2.0) were not met":
   libosmo-cc is missing or old. Do step 3, then run "./configure" again.

 * make: "undefined reference to osmo_cc_..." (for example
   osmo_cc_rtp_send_event): parts were built against another libosmo-cc. Run
   "make clean", "./configure" and "make" again.

 * configure: "syntax error near unexpected token `ALSA,'": pkg-config is
   missing. Install it (step 1) and run "autoreconf -if" and "./configure"
   again.

 * A program says "error while loading shared libraries: libosmocc.so":
   run "sudo ldconfig".

 * Several versions installed: "/sbin/ldconfig -p | grep osmocc" lists the
   libosmo-cc libraries the programs find.


Test
====

Sound card: run a B-Netz base station, it sends FSK tones:

  bnetz -k 17

SDR: check that SoapySDR finds the device, then run B-Netz on 153.330 MHz and
listen with a radio:

  SoapySDRUtil --find
  bnetz --limesdr -k 17        # LimeSDR
  bnetz --limesdr-mini -k 17   # LimeSDR Mini

Every program lists its options with -h. The radio setup, levels and
loopback tests are described at http://osmocom-analog.eversberg.eu/docs/


Calls through SIP
=================

Without other options a base station uses its built-in console: the mobile
calls the console, and the console can call the mobile. With -o the console is
off and the calls go out on the Osmo-CC socket (127.0.0.1, port 4200 or 4201,
whichever is free), where osmo-cc-sip-endpoint connects to it:

  nmt --limesdr -k 1 -k 4 -T CC/TC -T TC -0 1 -0 2 -Y se,1 -o
  osmo-cc-sip-endpoint --local 192.168.0.10 --remote 192.168.0.1

Both pick the free one of the two ports, start them in any order. For other
addresses use --cc "local <ip>:<port>" and --cc "remote <ip>:<port>" (see
--cc help). -x instead of -o connects mobiles with each other.


